<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seneca Falls Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seneca Falls Convention was the first women's rights convention, held in the Wesleyan Chapel at Seneca Falls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, on 19–20 July 1848. Organised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-cady-stanton">Elizabeth Cady Stanton</a> and local Quaker women during a visit by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretia">Lucretia</a> Mott, it adopted a Declaration of Sentiments modelled on the Declaration of Independence, signed by 100 of some 300 people present. Its resolution on women's suffrage passed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a> spoke for it. Stanton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susan-b-anthony">Susan B. Anthony</a> later presented it as the beginning of the women's rights movement, a view historians have since questioned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention.jpg" alt="Seneca Falls Convention" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woman's_Rights_Convention.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
